      <h1>Spec-First API Mocking: Run an OpenAPI Mock Server with Prism</h1>

      <div class="not-prose my-8 rounded-lg border border-indigo-200 bg-indigo-50/60 p-6 dark:border-indigo-500/30 dark:bg-indigo-500/5">
        <p class="mb-0 text-indigo-900/90 dark:text-indigo-200/90"><strong>An OpenAPI mock server turns your API description into a running HTTP server</strong> so frontends and tests can call real endpoints before the backend exists. Prism, from Stoplight (Apache-2.0), is the CLI most teams reach for: point it at an OpenAPI or Swagger file, or a Postman collection, and it listens on port 4010.</p>
      </div>

      <blockquote>
        <p><strong>Tested with</strong> <code>@stoplight/prism-cli</code> 5.16.0 on Node 22.23.3 (2026-10-15). Every curl response below comes from that run against the <code>users.yaml</code> in this guide. Mockoon CLI 9.9.0 was used for the comparison. We couldn't run Docker on the test machine; Docker commands follow Stoplight's docs and the <code>stoplight/prism</code> tags on Docker Hub. Lint the spec with <a href="/tools/spectral/">Spectral</a> first. devxtools.dev is independent and has no relationship with Stoplight or SmartBear.</p>
      </blockquote>

      <h2 id="tldr">TL;DR</h2>

      <p>Prism turns any OpenAPI 2/3.x file (or Postman collection) into a running mock server with one command. It returns your spec's examples or schema-generated data, and it rejects requests that break the contract. Use it when the <strong>spec is the source of truth</strong>. Pick <a href="/tools/mockoon/">Mockoon</a> or <a href="/tools/wiremock/">WireMock</a> when you need stateful or hand-tuned scenarios.</p>

      <h2 id="what-it-gives-you">What a spec-first mock server gives you</h2>

      <p>Three things, if the OpenAPI file is kept honest:</p>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>Frontend unblocked.</strong> The UI can call <code>/users</code> tomorrow, even if the real service ships next month.</li>
        <li><strong>Contract-shaped responses.</strong> Status codes, schemas and examples come from the same document the backend will implement.</li>
        <li><strong>Request validation.</strong> Callers that send a bad body or an out-of-range query parameter get a 422 before the real API exists, so client bugs show up early.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>The cost is that the mock is only as good as the spec. Stale examples produce stale UI. Pair Prism with a linter (<a href="/tools/spectral/">Spectral</a>) and, later, with contract tests (<a href="/tools/pact/">Pact</a>) or a validation proxy (covered below).</p>

      <h2 id="quick-start">Quick start</h2>

      <pre><code class="language-bash">{`npm i -D @stoplight/prism-cli   # 5.16.0 at the time of writing; declares Node >=24.18.0
# or, one-shot:
npx @stoplight/prism-cli mock openapi.yaml`}</code></pre>

      <p>Prism 5.16.0 declares Node &gt;=24.18.0 in its <code>engines</code> field. Our runs used Node 22.23.3, and a basic mock also worked on 20.19.2 and 24.22.0, but use Node 24.18+ for a supported setup. Prism listens on <code>http://127.0.0.1:4010</code> by default (<code>-p</code> / <code>-h</code> change that). CORS is on by default.</p>

      <h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>

      <h3>What is an OpenAPI mock server?</h3>
      <p>A process that reads an OpenAPI (or Swagger) document and exposes the described paths over HTTP, returning examples or schema-generated bodies so clients can develop and test against the contract before the real API exists.</p>

      <h3>How do I mock an API from an OpenAPI spec?</h3>
      <p>Install Prism (<code>npm i -D @stoplight/prism-cli</code>) and run <code>npx prism mock openapi.yaml</code>. It listens on port 4010. Use <code>Prefer: example=…</code>, <code>Prefer: code=…</code> or <code>--dynamic</code> to pick responses.</p>

      <h3>Is Prism free?</h3>
      <p>Yes. The CLI and Docker image are Apache-2.0 open source (~227K weekly npm downloads for <code>@stoplight/prism-cli</code> in the week of 2026-10-06 to 2026-10-12). Stoplight also sells commercial API-design products that are separate from Prism.</p>

      <h3>Can Prism return random data?</h3>
      <p>Yes. Pass <code>-d</code> / <code>--dynamic</code>, or send <code>Prefer: dynamic=true</code>. Responses are built with json-schema-faker; <code>x-faker</code> on a property selects a Faker method. Use <code>--seed</code> and <code>--json-schema-faker-fillProperties=false</code> for more control.</p>

      <h3>How do I run Prism in Docker?</h3>
      <p><code>docker run --init --rm -v $(pwd):/tmp -p 4010:4010 stoplight/prism:5 mock -h 0.0.0.0 /tmp/openapi.yaml</code>. Pin a major tag, and bind to <code>0.0.0.0</code> so the port is reachable from outside the container.</p>

      <h3>Prism vs Mockoon: which should I use?</h3>
      <p>Use Prism when OpenAPI is the source of truth and you want request validation (and the validation proxy) for free. Use Mockoon when you want a desktop GUI, templating, or limited stateful CRUD without maintaining a full schema for every edge case.</p>

